  "worked_solution": "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{\u2033}+4y} & =L{8{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2(t-\\pi ))} \\\\ L{{y}^{\u2033}}+4L{y} & =L{8{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2(t-\\pi ))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{\u2033}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{\u2033}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{\u2032}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand side, we use the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nHere, $a=\\pi $ and $f(t-\\pi )=8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2(t-\\pi ))$, so $f(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t).$ The transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)}=8\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\frac{16}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nApplying the shift, we get\n\n$$\nL{8{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2(t-\\pi ))}={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot \\frac{16}{{s}^{2}+4}=\\frac{16{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{16{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{16{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{16{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+4)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
